Q: Jump in Unity using Rigidbody Force A: <p>The problem is that you check for landing in the same function as the jump and instantly set  <code>isGrounded</code> to false. You must use a proper ground detection mechanism. Here are some of the most common:</p>
<h3>1. <strong><a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/Collider.OnCollisionEnter.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">OnCollisionEnter</a>:</strong></h3>
<p>For this to work, you need to add the <strong>&quot;Ground&quot;</strong> tag to your floor objects.</p>
<pre><code>private void OnCollisionEnter(Collision collision)
{
    // Check if the object we touched is tagged as Ground
    if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag(&quot;Ground&quot;))
    {
        isGrounded = true;
    }
}

public void Jump()
{
    if (isGrounded)
    {
        playerRigidbody.AddForce(Vector3.up * jumpForce, ForceMode.Impulse);
        isGrounded = false; 
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>2. <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/Physics.Raycast.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Physics Raycast</a>:</h3>
<p>You can do this with one of these options: inside <code>Jump()</code> or, more commonly, in <code>FixedUpdate</code> for constant monitoring.</p>
<pre><code>// Option A:
private void FixedUpdate()
{
    isGrounded = Physics.Raycast(transform.position, Vector3.down, 0.6f);
}

public void Jump()
{
    if (isGrounded) 
    {
        playerRigidbody.AddForce(Vector3.up * jumpForce, ForceMode.Impulse);
    }
}

...

// Option B:
public void Jump()
{
    isGrounded = Physics.Raycast(transform.position, Vector3.down, 0.6f);
    if (isGrounded) 
    {
        playerRigidbody.AddForce(Vector3.up * jumpForce, ForceMode.Impulse);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3>3. <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/CharacterController-isGrounded.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">CharacterController</a>:</h3>
<p>To use this property, you would need to replace your Rigidbody component with a <code>CharacterController</code> and rewrite your movement logic to work with its specialized physics system.</p>
<pre><code>public void Jump()
{
    // CharacterController handles the grounding logic automatically
    if (myController.isGrounded)
    {
        // Jump logic (usually by modifying vertical velocity)
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Scanlines gets wrong pixel colors A: <p>I tried your code, and your image, and it works for me.</p>
<p>So my guess is that your image is actually a 32-bit bitmap, and not a 24-bit bitmap, as your code expects. (The actual pixel format of your bitmap was obviously lost when you uploaded it as a PNG. And then I saved it as a 24-bit bitmap.)</p>
<p>You need to check the <code>PixelFormat</code> of your bitmap. Your code will only work if this is <code>pf24bit</code>.</p>
<p>Also, you don't need to create your own pixel records. Just use <code>TRGBTriple</code> or <code>TRGBQuad</code>, depending on the pixel format.</p>
<p>Here's some sample code:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>function GetAverageColor(ABitmap: TBitmap): TColor;
begin

  {$POINTERMATH ON}

  var r: Double := 0.0;
  var g: Double := 0.0;
  var b: Double := 0.0;

  case ABitmap.PixelFormat of
    pf24bit:
      begin

        for var y := 0 to ABitmap.Height - 1 do
        begin
          const SL: PRGBTriple = ABitmap.ScanLine[y];
          for var x := 0 to ABitmap.Width - 1 do
          begin
            r := r + SL[x].rgbtRed / 255;
            g := g + SL[x].rgbtGreen / 255;
            b := b + SL[x].rgbtBlue / 255;
          end;
        end;

      end;
    pf32bit:
      begin

        for var y := 0 to ABitmap.Height - 1 do
        begin
          const SL: PRGBQuad = ABitmap.ScanLine[y];
          for var x := 0 to ABitmap.Width - 1 do
          begin
            r := r + SL[x].rgbRed / 255;
            g := g + SL[x].rgbGreen / 255;
            b := b + SL[x].rgbBlue / 255;
          end;
        end;

      end;
  else
    raise Exception.Create('Unsupported pixel format.');
  end;

  r := r / (ABitmap.Width * ABitmap.Height);
  g := g / (ABitmap.Width * ABitmap.Height);
  b := b / (ABitmap.Width * ABitmap.Height);

  Result := RGB(
    Round(255 * r),
    Round(255 * g),
    Round(255 * b)
  );

end;
</code></pre>
<p>In addition, I would not use a <code>TImage</code> for this, but a custom control (or just a plain <code>TForm</code>) and my own off-screen <code>TBitmap</code> (which I set to <code>pf24bit</code>).</p>
<p>Also, I'd make sure to double-buffer and handle the <code>WM_ERASEBKGND</code> message to remove flickering.</p>
<p>And you can use <code>csLButtonDown in ControlState</code> instead of your own flag.</p>
<h1>Complete example</h1>
<p>Here's how I'd do this:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>unit Unit1;

interface

uses
  Winapi.Windows, Winapi.Messages, System.SysUtils, System.Variants, System.Classes, Vcl.Graphics,
  Vcl.Controls, Vcl.Forms, Vcl.Dialogs;

type
  TForm1 = class(TForm)
    procedure FormCreate(Sender: TObject);
    procedure FormPaint(Sender: TObject);
    procedure FormMouseDown(Sender: TObject; Button: TMouseButton;
      Shift: TShiftState; X, Y: Integer);
    procedure FormMouseMove(Sender: TObject; Shift: TShiftState; X, Y: Integer);
    procedure FormMouseUp(Sender: TObject; Button: TMouseButton;
      Shift: TShiftState; X, Y: Integer);
  private
    FBitmap: TBitmap;
    FStart,
    FEnd: TPoint;
    procedure DoFillRect(const ARect: TRect; AColor: TColor);
  public
    procedure WMEraseBkgnd(var Message: TWMEraseBkgnd); message WM_ERASEBKGND;
  end;

var
  Form1: TForm1;

implementation

{$R *.dfm}

function GetAverageColor(ABitmap: TBitmap; const ARect: TRect): TColor;
begin

  {$POINTERMATH ON}

  var r: Double := 0.0;
  var g: Double := 0.0;
  var b: Double := 0.0;

  Assert(Rect(0, 0, ABitmap.Width - 1, ABitmap.Height - 1).Contains(ARect));
  Assert(not ARect.IsEmpty);

  case ABitmap.PixelFormat of
    pf24bit:
      begin

        for var y := ARect.Top to ARect.Bottom - 1 do
        begin
          const SL: PRGBTriple = ABitmap.ScanLine[y];
          for var x := ARect.Left to ARect.Right - 1 do
          begin
            r := r + SL[x].rgbtRed / 255;
            g := g + SL[x].rgbtGreen / 255;
            b := b + SL[x].rgbtBlue / 255;
          end;
        end;

      end;
    pf32bit:
      begin

        for var y := ARect.Top to ARect.Bottom - 1 do
        begin
          const SL: PRGBQuad = ABitmap.ScanLine[y];
          for var x := ARect.Left to ARect.Right - 1 do
          begin
            r := r + SL[x].rgbBlue / 255;
            g := g + SL[x].rgbGreen / 255;
            b := b + SL[x].rgbBlue / 255;
          end;
        end;

      end;
  else
    raise Exception.Create('Unsupported pixel format.');
  end;

  const A = ARect.Width * ARect.Height;
  r := r / A;
  g := g / A;
  b := b / A;

  Result := RGB(
    Round(255 * r),
    Round(255 * g),
    Round(255 * b)
  );

end;

procedure TForm1.DoFillRect(const ARect: TRect; AColor: TColor);
begin

  if FBitmap = nil then
    Exit;

  FBitmap.Canvas.Brush.Color := AColor;
  FBitmap.Canvas.Brush.Style := bsSolid;
  FBitmap.Canvas.FillRect(ARect);

end;

procedure TForm1.FormCreate(Sender: TObject);
begin

  DoubleBuffered := True;
  Cursor := crCross;

  FBitmap := TBitmap.Create;
  FBitmap.LoadFromFile('D:\test.bmp');
  FBitmap.PixelFormat := pf24bit;

end;

procedure TForm1.FormMouseDown(Sender: TObject; Button: TMouseButton;
  Shift: TShiftState; X, Y: Integer);
begin
  if Button = TMouseButton.mbLeft then
  begin
    FStart := Point(X, Y);
    var R := ClientToScreen(Rect(0, 0, FBitmap.Width - 1, FBitmap.Height - 1));
    ClipCursor(@R);
  end;
end;

procedure TForm1.FormMouseMove(Sender: TObject; Shift: TShiftState; X,
  Y: Integer);
begin

  if csLButtonDown in ControlState then
  begin
    FEnd := Point(X, Y);
    Invalidate;
  end;

end;

procedure TForm1.FormMouseUp(Sender: TObject; Button: TMouseButton;
  Shift: TShiftState; X, Y: Integer);
begin

  ClipCursor(nil);

  var R := Rect(FStart, FEnd);
  R.NormalizeRect;

  FStart := TPoint.Zero;
  FEnd := TPoint.Zero;

  if Button = TMouseButton.mbLeft then
  begin

    if not R.IsEmpty then
      DoFillRect(R, GetAverageColor(FBitmap, R));

    Invalidate;

  end;

end;

procedure TForm1.FormPaint(Sender: TObject);
begin

  Canvas.Brush.Color := Self.Color;
  Canvas.Brush.Style := bsSolid;
  Canvas.FillRect(ClientRect);

  if Assigned(FBitmap) then
    Canvas.Draw(0, 0, FBitmap);

  if csLButtonDown in ControlState then
  begin
    var R := Rect(FStart, FEnd);
    R.NormalizeRect;
    Canvas.Pen.Mode := pmNot;
    Canvas.Brush.Style := bsClear;
    Canvas.Rectangle(R);
    Canvas.Pen.Mode := pmBlack;
  end;

end;

procedure TForm1.WMEraseBkgnd(var Message: TWMEraseBkgnd);
begin
  Message.Result := 1;
end;

end.
</code></pre>
<p>Example:</p>
<p><a href="https://privat.rejbrand.se/avgbitmapcolor.mp4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://privat.rejbrand.se/avgbitmapcolor.mp4</a></p>

Q: Puppeteer case insensitive text locator with variable from Node scope A: <p>The root issue, which I think you understand, is that these functions don't run in Node context, they run in the browser context--the page being automated. So all scope from Node is unavailable.</p>
<p>One option is to pass a string in to <code>.filter()</code> instead of a function:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const puppeteer = require(&quot;puppeteer&quot;); // ^24.4.0

const html = `&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  document.body.innerHTML = &quot;&lt;a&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;&quot;;
}, 3000);
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;`;

let browser;
(async () =&gt; {
  browser = await puppeteer.launch();
  const [page] = await browser.pages();
  await page.setContent(html);
  const target = &quot;about&quot;;
  const el = await page
    .locator(&quot;a&quot;)
    .filter(`el =&gt; el.textContent.toLowerCase() === &quot;${target}&quot;`)
    .waitHandle();
  console.log(await el.evaluate(el =&gt; el.textContent)); // =&gt; About
})()
  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err))
  .finally(() =&gt; browser?.close());
</code></pre>
<p>(google.com has robot blocking, so using a hardcoded HTML string is more reproducible)</p>
<p>Alternately, you can use <code>waitForFunction</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const el = await page.waitForFunction(
  target =&gt;
    [...document.querySelectorAll(&quot;a&quot;)].find(
      el =&gt; el.textContent.toLowerCase() === target
    ),
  {},
  target
);
</code></pre>
<p>Or, yet again, a string, since you have a simple one-line function which is amenable to this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const el = await page.waitForFunction(`
  [...document.querySelectorAll(&quot;a&quot;)].find(
    el =&gt; el.textContent.toLowerCase() === &quot;${target}&quot;
  )
`);
</code></pre>
<p><code>evaluateHandle</code> doesn't auto-wait and is discouraged, but just as a proof of concept, here's how you can use it:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const el = await page.evaluateHandle(
  (target) =&gt;
    [...document.querySelectorAll(&quot;a&quot;)].find(el =&gt;
      el.textContent.toLowerCase() === target.toLowerCase() // not a regex
    ),
  aboutText4 // &lt;-- use the second argument to pass data into the browser
);
</code></pre>
<p>I would also call <code>.trim()</code> after <code>.toLowerCase()</code>.</p>
<p>See also <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/47407791/how-do-you-click-on-an-element-with-text-in-puppeteer/64626061#64626061">How do you click on an element with text in Puppeteer?</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79882866/locator-filter-works-with-hardcoded-value-but-not-variable/79882931#79882931">locator.filter works with hardcoded value but not variable</a>.</p>
